From: OpenWrt
Subject: [PATCH] mt7615: allow forcing DBDC from the device tree

mt7615_eeprom_parse_hw_band_cap() derives the band configuration purely
from MT_EE_WIFI_CONF (EEPROM offset 0x3e, bits 5:4). Some boards are
wired for DBDC yet report MT_EE_DUAL_BAND there, because their vendor
driver turns DBDC on unconditionally and never consults the EEPROM.
On such boards mt76 registers a single dual-band phy and only one band
is usable at a time.

Add a "mediatek,dbdc" boolean property so the device tree can override
the EEPROM value, which is what the ipTIME A3004NS-M (MT7615D) needs.
Behaviour is unchanged for every device that does not set the property.

--- a/mt7615/eeprom.c
+++ b/mt7615/eeprom.c
@@ -124,6 +124,19 @@ mt7615_eeprom_parse_hw_band_cap(struct mt7615_dev *dev)
 
 	val = FIELD_GET(MT_EE_NIC_WIFI_CONF_BAND_SEL,
 			eeprom[MT_EE_WIFI_CONF]);
+
+	/*
+	 * Some boards wire the chip up for DBDC but ship an EEPROM that still
+	 * describes it as a single dual-band phy. The vendor driver enables
+	 * DBDC unconditionally, so the mismatch goes unnoticed there; here it
+	 * means only one band ever comes up. Let the device tree correct it.
+	 *
+	 * Known affected device: ipTIME A3004NS-M (MT7615D, band_sel reads
+	 * MT_EE_DUAL_BAND).
+	 */
+	if (of_property_read_bool(dev_of_node(dev->mt76.dev), "mediatek,dbdc"))
+		val = MT_EE_DBDC;
+
 	switch (val) {
 	case MT_EE_5GHZ:
 		dev->mphy.cap.has_5ghz = true;
